---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release111 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="browser-agent">

          <p>
            The browser is a real Chromium view embedded in the Vector window, not a screenshot service, and it is
            attached to the current repository and session. Open a local preview or URL, interact manually, or ask
            Vector to inspect and test the page as part of the engineering task.
          </p>

          <h2 id="controlled-browser">Observe, repair, retest</h2>
          <p>
            The agent opens the running product, clicks and types, reads console and network failures, captures what
            it saw, repairs the code, and retests.
          </p>

          <h2 id="sensitive-steps">Sign-in and sensitive steps</h2>
          <p>
            The agent refuses to type into password, one-time-code and card fields. It pauses and hands the live
            browser to you, then resumes with what it can see.
          </p>
          <div class="do-dont">
            <article>
              <strong>Good uses</strong>
              <p>Local UI testing, console inspection, screenshots, documentation research, and approved deployment workflows.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <strong>Approval stays visible</strong>
              <p>Take over for sign-in, secrets, payments, destructive changes, and other sensitive actions.</p>
            </article>
          </div>

          <h2 id="reading-pages">Reading a page, and handing it to you</h2>
          <p>
            {release111.since} the agent reads a page's main text with its links, lists its links, finds text on it, and
            sees a short outline of what it can click or type into. When a page needs your sign-in, or a paywall you can
            get past, the agent hands it to you in Preview: <em>Sign in or get past the paywall yourself in Preview, then
            press Continue.</em> It then reads the page you reached.
          </p>

          <h2 id="page-limits">What a page may do</h2>
          <p>
            {release111.since} the agent browser grants a page no permission but fullscreen: no camera, microphone,
            location, notifications, clipboard or devices, and it never asks you on a page's behalf. Downloads are
            cancelled: <em>Downloads are off in the agent browser.</em> Approving a site with Always allow covers that
            site, never every site. Everything the agent reads from a page reaches the model fenced as untrusted, with
            hidden text removed and lines addressed to AI assistants flagged.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/docs/research#research-browsers">Deep research</a> gives each research agent a browser of its own,
            apart from this one and from each other.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
